diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..bcfeb42
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,612 @@
+# Helpdesk API
+
+JSON backend for a multi-business helpdesk SaaS (Rails 8.1, API-only, PostgreSQL).
+The frontend is a separate app.
+
+## Setup
+
+```sh
+bin/setup --skip-server        # bundle, create + migrate the database
+bin/rails server               # http://localhost:3000
+bin/rails test                 # creates the test database on first run
+```
+
+### Configuration
+
+| Variable | Default | Purpose |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `DATABASE_URL` | – | PostgreSQL connection. Tests use `TEST_DATABASE_URL`, or the same server with the database name suffixed `_test` (`helpdesk_development` → `helpdesk_test`), so the test suite never wipes your data. |
+| `HELPDESK_FRONTEND_URL` | `http://localhost:3001` | Base URL for links in emails; its origin is the one allowed by CORS. |
+| `HELPDESK_MAIL_FROM` | `support@example.test` | Sender of all outgoing email. |
+| `SMTP_ADDRESS`, `SMTP_PORT` (587), `SMTP_USERNAME`, `SMTP_PASSWORD`, `SMTP_DOMAIN`, `SMTP_AUTHENTICATION`, `SMTP_ENABLE_STARTTLS_AUTO` (true) | – | SMTP delivery for Action Mailer. Without them, development just logs each message (including its links) to `log/development.log`. |
+| `HELPDESK_SUPPORT_DOMAIN` | `support.example.test` | Domain at which businesses receive email: Acme's support address is `acme@support.example.test`. See [Email](#email). |
+| `RAILS_INBOUND_EMAIL_PASSWORD` | – | Password the mail server uses for the inbound email ingress. Required to receive email. |
+| `HELPDESK_STORAGE_PATH` | `storage/` | Where received emails are kept until they're deleted (development and production). Use a persistent volume. |
+| `SECRET_KEY_BASE` | – | Required in production. |
+| `RAILS_FORCE_SSL` / `RAILS_ASSUME_SSL` | `true` | Production TLS behaviour. |
+
+### Cleanup of cancelled accounts
+
+```sh
+bin/rails helpdesk:purge_cancelled_accounts
+```
+
+Permanently deletes every account that has been cancelled for 90 days or more,
+together with all of its data (users, sessions, tokens, tickets and their
+comments; every table cascades from `accounts`). Run it daily, e.g. from cron.
+
+### Received email
+
+```sh
+bin/rails helpdesk:incinerate_inbound_emails   # daily
+bin/rails helpdesk:process_inbound_emails      # every few minutes
+```
+
+The source of every received email is kept (in Active Storage) for 30 days
+after it was processed; the first task then deletes it. Received emails are
+processed by a job, and there's no persistent job backend yet (jobs run in the
+server process), so the second task processes any email whose job was lost in
+a restart: those still waiting after 10 minutes. Processing an email twice is
+harmless.
+
+## API conventions
+
+* Base path `/api/v1`; JSON request and response bodies. Request bodies use a
+  named envelope, e.g. `{"session": {...}}`.
+* **Choosing the business:** send its subdomain in `X-Helpdesk-Account: acme`.
+  It is required for public business endpoints (portal, sign-in, requesting
+  emailed links). Signed-in requests are bound to the session's business; the
+  header is optional there but must match if sent.
+* **Authentication:** `Authorization: Bearer TOKEN`, where the token comes from
+  `POST /sessions`. Sessions last 30 days. Only a SHA-256 digest of each token is
+  stored.
+* **Errors** always look like:
+
+  ```json
+  {"error": {"code": "validation_failed", "message": "Subdomain has already been taken",
+             "details": {"subdomain": ["has already been taken"]}}}
+  ```
+
+  | Status | Codes |
+  | --- | --- |
+  | 400 | `parameter_missing`, `malformed_request`, `account_required` |
+  | 401 | `unauthorized` (missing/invalid/expired token), `invalid_credentials` |
+  | 403 | `email_not_verified`, `user_suspended`, `account_cancelled`, `forbidden` |
+  | 404 | `account_not_found`, `not_found` |
+  | 422 | `validation_failed`, `invalid_token`, `invalid_current_password`, `account_already_cancelled`, `account_not_cancelled`, `reactivation_period_expired`, `invitation_already_accepted`, `invitation_revoked`, `ticket_closed` |
+  | 429 | `rate_limited` (with `Retry-After`) |
+
+### Resources
+
+```json
+"account": {"id": 1, "name": "Acme", "subdomain": "acme", "status": "active", "owner_id": 1,
+            "cancelled_at": null, "reactivatable_until": null,
+            "created_at": "2025-01-01T12:00:00.000Z", "updated_at": "2025-01-01T12:00:00.000Z"}
+"user":    {"id": 1, "account_id": 1, "name": "Jo", "email": "jo@acme.test", "role": "admin",
+            "owner": true, "verified": false, "email_verified_at": null,
+            "suspended": false, "suspended_at": null, "created_at": "...", "updated_at": "..."}
+"session": {"id": 1, "token": "…", "token_type": "Bearer", "expires_at": "...", "created_at": "..."}
+"portal":  {"name": "Acme", "subdomain": "acme"}
+"request": {"id": 42, "subject": "Cannot download my receipt", "status": "open", "merged_into_id": null,
+            "requester_id": 10, "created_at": "...", "updated_at": "..."}
+"ticket":  {"id": 42, "subject": "Cannot download my receipt", "status": "open", "merged_into_id": null,
+            "priority": "normal", "requester_id": 10, "requester": {"id": 10, "name": "Avery", "email": "avery@example.test"},
+            "assignee_id": 12, "assignee": {"id": 12, "name": "Sam"},
+            "group_id": 20, "group": {"id": 20, "name": "Billing"},
+            "created_at": "...", "updated_at": "..."}
+"comment": {"id": 7, "body": "The download button does nothing.", "author": {"id": 10, "name": "Avery"},
+            "public": true, "created_at": "..."}
+"staff":   {"id": 12, "name": "Sam", "email": "sam@example.test", "role": "agent"}
+"group":   {"id": 20, "account_id": 1, "name": "Billing", "created_at": "...", "updated_at": "..."}
+"group_membership": {"id": 5, "group_id": 20, "group": {"id": 20, "name": "Billing"},
+                     "user_id": 12, "user": {"id": 12, "name": "Sam"}, "default": true,
+                     "created_at": "...", "updated_at": "..."}
+"invitation": {"id": 3, "account_id": 1, "email": "sam@example.test", "role": "agent", "status": "pending",
+               "invited_by_id": 1, "invited_by": {"id": 1, "name": "Jo"}, "user_id": null,
+               "sent_at": "...", "expires_at": "...", "accepted_at": null, "revoked_at": null,
+               "created_at": "...", "updated_at": "..."}
+"invitation_preview": {"email": "sam@example.test", "role": "agent", "account_name": "Acme", "subdomain": "acme",
+                       "account": {"name": "Acme", "subdomain": "acme"}, "invited_by": {"name": "Jo"},
+                       "expires_at": "..."}
+```
+
+A ticket's `assignee` is `null` when nobody is assigned, and its `group` is
+`null` when it isn't in a [group](#groups). A ticket's or request's
+`merged_into_id` is the ticket it was [merged into](#merging-tickets), or
+`null` if it wasn't merged. Its `priority` is `normal` or
+`high`. Priority, assignee and group are internal, so requests don't show
+them. An invitation's `user_id` is the user who accepted it, and `sent_at` is
+when the latest link was emailed.
+
+A user's `role` is `admin` or `agent` (the business's **staff**) or `customer`.
+Both kinds of staff work on the business's tickets; only admins manage people
+(users and invitations) and [groups](#groups). Requests and tickets are the
+same records: customers see their own as `request`s, and staff see all of the
+business's as `ticket`s.
+
+### Pagination
+
+Every list can be paged by page number or by cursor. The records always come
+under the plural name, in the list's usual order.
+
+**Page numbers** use `page` and `per_page` (defaults 1 and 25; at most 100;
+missing or invalid values use the defaults), with the pagination fields
+alongside the records:
+
+```json
+{"tickets": [...], "count": 60, "page": 2, "per_page": 25, "next_page": 3, "previous_page": 1}
+```
+
+`count` is the total number of records, and `next_page` / `previous_page` are
+page numbers, or `null` at either end. Pages are counted from the start of the
+list every time, so they get slower deeper into a long list and shift when
+records are added, or move up (such as a ticket that's updated).
+
+**Cursors** don't have either problem: every page costs the same however deep
+it is, and navigation stays put while other people work. Start with
+`page[size]` (default 25; at most 100; missing or invalid values use the
+default), plus any filters:
+
+```http
+GET /api/v1/tickets?page[size]=25&status=open
+```
+
+```json
+{"tickets": [...],
+ "meta": {"has_more": true, "after_cursor": "WyI2ZDdi…", "before_cursor": "WyI2ZDdi…"},
+ "links": {"next": "https://api.example.test/api/v1/tickets?page%5Bafter%5D=WyI2ZDdi…&page%5Bsize%5D=25&status=open",
+           "prev": null}}
+```
+
+* `after_cursor` is the cursor of the page's last record and `before_cursor`
+  that of its first. Send one back as `page[after]` (the records after it) or
+  `page[before]` (the records before it) with `page[size]` and the same
+  filters. Or just follow `links.next` / `links.prev`, which are full URLs that
+  do exactly that.
+* `has_more` says whether the list goes on in the direction you're browsing:
+  after the page for the first page and for `page[after]`, before it for
+  `page[before]`.
+* `links.next` / `links.prev` are `null` when there's nothing after / before
+  the page. The first page has no `prev`.
+* An empty page has neither cursors nor links:
+  `{"tickets": [], "meta": {"has_more": false, "after_cursor": null, "before_cursor": null}, "links": {"next": null, "prev": null}}`.
+* A cursor holds the position of its record as it was when the page was
+  served. Pages carry on from there whatever happens meanwhile: records that
+  are added, removed or that move (a ticket that's updated goes to the top of
+  `/tickets`) never make the rest repeat or get skipped, and a cursor still
+  works after its own record changed or was deleted. Records that moved show
+  up where they are now, so you see them when you go back that way.
+* Cursors are opaque: don't build or change them. A cursor works with the list
+  it came from, also with other filters. A malformed cursor or one from a list
+  in another order is a `422 validation_failed` with
+  `{"page[after]": ["is not a valid cursor"]}` (or `page[before]`); start
+  again from the first page. Sending both `page[after]` and `page[before]` is
+  a `422` with `{"page": ["can't have both after and before"]}`. An empty
+  cursor is the same as none.
+* Cursor pages don't have `count`. A request uses one kind of pagination:
+  `page[...]` parameters mean cursors, and `page=2` together with
+  `page[size]` is a `400`.
+
+## Endpoints
+
+| Method & path | Auth | Body | Success |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| `POST /account/create` | – | `{"account": {"name", "subdomain", "admin": {"name", "email", "password"}}}` | 201 `account`, `user` |
+| `GET /portal` | header | – | 200 `portal` |
+| `POST /registrations` | header | `{"user": {"name", "email", "password"}}` | 201 `user` |
+| `POST /sessions` | header | `{"session": {"email", "password"}}` | 201 `session`, `user`, `account` |
+| `DELETE /session` | token | – | 204 |
+| `GET /me` | token | – | 200 `user` |
+| `PATCH /me` | token | `{"user": {"name"}}` | 200 `user` |
+| `PUT /me/password` | token | `{"password_change": {"current_password", "password"}}` | 200 `user`, `session` |
